A||||3||Eric||3.4 Swap
Z||000000||Eric||07-01-99||12:48 PM||||I'm looking into doing a 3.4 motor swap.  I have decided to "not screw"  with the 2.8/3.2.  The cost of modifying the 2.8 would be nearly the same as an engine from a junked car and there would be a lot less work involved.  I know I read a post from someone who has done this.  <p>There are several types of 3.4 motors that will bolt up to the cradle without any major modifications.  The DOHC motor makes the most power (210hp), but I think I may be better off with the old style pushrod motor for future performance mods.  I hope some of you can tell me some good/bad things about this.  Am I crazy for even considering it?
Z||000001||Ray Heath||07-01-99||02:25 PM||||I am building a 3.4, the engine is in the machine shop at this time. There is some good information you need  to read before you get started. You can find it at the Fiero Factory (Fierofactory@juno.com) You can reach Ed at 256-420-5391...
Z||000002||batboy||07-01-99||08:21 PM||||I think the 3.4 is a good engine, the main drawback is that there is a fair amount of modification and expense involved. If you are up to the task and have a few bucks, it'll probably work out pretty well. Like Ray said, Ed Parks is the best authority that I know about the swap. There is a web site that explains what needs to be done, but I can't find it off-hand. I think you have to relocate the starter from one side to the other and a few things like that. Somewhere here in this forum is some info too. With the right modifications, the 3.4 can produce more torque than the 2.8 or 3.1 engines. Good luck.
Z||000003||Eric||07-02-99||04:51 PM||||I changed my mind!  I've done some research on the 3.4 and have found that there is not much more power in the stock motor than a 2./3.1.  Besides, now I know what's involved with a 3.2 build.  It is just going to take some more time.  I don't like the thought of my Fiero sitting in the garage, but it has already been there for a month and then a month before that and then a month before that.  Now that I think about it, my car has been parked more days than it has been driven since I've owned it (5 months).
